-### [Clipping Distance Computation via Volume Corners](2622-clipping-distance-computation-via-volume-corners.md)
-Computes the near and far clipping distances for ray marching by projecting the 8 corners of the volume bounding box onto the camera view direction. It extracts the camera position and view direction from the camera matrix (handling world-to-camera vs camera-to-world inversion via a boolean mask to avoid branching). It generates the 8 corners of the volume in IJK space, transforms them to RAS space using the `ijk2ras` matrix, and projects the vector from the camera to each corner onto the normalized view direction. The near distance is set to the minimum projected distance minus a margin, and the far distance to the maximum plus the margin.
+Computes the bounding box in Normalized Device Coordinates (NDC) space from a set of rays [S6, unofficial]. It computes the minimum and maximum of ray origins and endpoints (near and far planes) and returns the stacked min/max bounds: `torch.stack((torch.minimum(near_min, far_min), torch.maximum(near_max, far_max)))` [S6, unofficial]. This is used when rendering in NDC space where the bounding box is axis-aligned in the normalized cube [-1, 1] or similar [S6, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** $O(N \cdot S)$, where $N$ is the number of rays and $S$ is the average number of samples per ray [G]. The intersection calculation is $O(N)$, and point generation is $O(N \cdot S)$ [G].
-- **Memory Complexity:** $O(N \cdot S)$ to store the generated points, masks, and indices [G].
-- **Performance:** The method is highly parallelizable and is typically implemented in CUDA for performance. Batching rays (e.g., 10,000 per batch) helps manage GPU memory usage [S3, unofficial].
-- **Strengths:** Ensures samples are only generated within the valid scene volume, reducing unnecessary computations for empty space [S3, unofficial]. Handles edge cases like zero-direction rays and near-far clipping robustly [S3, unofficial].
-- **Limitations:** The fixed step size may lead to aliasing if the step size is too large relative to the scene features. The method assumes an axis-aligned bounding box, which may not tightly fit rotated or irregular geometries [G].
-- **When to Use:** When rendering volumetric data within a known bounding box, especially in coarse geometry search phases or when using fixed-step ray marching [S3, unofficial].
-- **When Not to Use:** When the scene geometry is highly irregular and a tight bounding box is not available, or when adaptive step sizes are required for high-frequency details [G].
